From 38906124a688bccc33d139537e5dbfd406420abd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: terminills <tdeters@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 12:50:17 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 608/638] Honour PageAccessibilityDisposition in the AROS
 decommit path

DecommitSystemPagesInternal() ignored its PageAccessibilityDisposition
parameter - it was not even named - and always stripped access to zero. That
is stricter than the platform it emulates. page_allocator_internals_posix.h
computes

    change_permissions = (disposition == kRequireUpdate)

and then makes pages inaccessible "unless the caller requested to keep
permissions", and the enum documents kAllowKeepForPerf as "will not update
permissions, if the platform supports that (POSIX & Fuchsia only)". So a
caller that decommits kAllowKeepForPerf and subsequently reads the range is
fine on Linux, where the pages stay mapped, and faults here.

This is not a rare path. Instrumenting the two dispositions separately shows
one page load of a real site doing 122 kAllowKeepForPerf decommits against 6
kRequireUpdate ones, so the old code was performing 122 protection strips per
load that the caller had explicitly said were unnecessary - and leaving 122
ranges unreadable that POSIX would have left readable.

The zeroing stays on both paths: DiscardSystemPagesInternal() is a no-op on
AROS, so that memset is what emulates the discard, and a reader of a
kAllowKeepForPerf range now sees zeroes - which is what a POSIX caller gets
back after MADV_DONTNEED.

The trace now distinguishes "decommit" from "decommit-keep" so a faulting
address can be attributed to the disposition that covered it.

THIS DOES NOT FIX THE CRASH IT WAS FOUND WHILE CHASING, and the new trace is
what proves it. Loading https://aros.sourceforge.io/ still page-faults
(TRAPNO 0x0e, ERR 0x4, a read) and the faulting address is covered by
kRequireUpdate decommits:

    CR2  0x00007f83c18bfff0
    #149 decommit [0x7f83c1800000, 0x7f83c1a00000)
    #151 decommit [0x7f83c1800000, 0x7f83c1a00000)
    #152 decommit [0x7f83c1800000, 0x7f83c1c00000)

i.e. memory PartitionAlloc deliberately made inaccessible is still being read,
which is a genuine use-after-decommit somewhere above the page allocator, not
a permissions-semantics difference. Committing this separately so that bug is
chased against correct platform semantics rather than through an extra layer
of divergence.

I had also suspected the repeated identical decommits of one 2 MB range were
evidence of lost bookkeeping. They are not: this implementation makes decommit
idempotent, so repeats are harmless.
---
 .../page_allocator_internals_aros.h           | 25 ++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 22 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/base/allocator/partition_allocator/src/partition_alloc/page_allocator_internals_aros.h b/base/allocator/partition_allocator/src/partition_alloc/page_allocator_internals_aros.h
index 50eb6ceafb..c43aea1aa4 100644
--- a/base/allocator/partition_allocator/src/partition_alloc/page_allocator_internals_aros.h
+++ b/base/allocator/partition_allocator/src/partition_alloc/page_allocator_internals_aros.h
@@ -553,14 +553,33 @@ uintptr_t TrimMappingInternal(uintptr_t base_address,
 void DecommitSystemPagesInternal(
     uintptr_t address,
     size_t length,
-    PageAccessibilityDisposition) {
+    PageAccessibilityDisposition accessibility_disposition) {
   APTR KernelBase = OpenKernelResource();
   PA_CHECK(KernelBase);
-  TracePageEvent("decommit", address, length, 0);
+  // Honour the disposition, as POSIX does. page_allocator_internals_posix.h
+  // computes the same flag and then makes pages inaccessible "unless the
+  // caller requested to keep permissions", and the enum itself documents
+  // kAllowKeepForPerf as "will not update permissions, if the platform
+  // supports that (POSIX & Fuchsia only)".
+  //
+  // This function previously ignored the parameter and always stripped access
+  // to zero, which is STRICTER than the platform it emulates: a caller that
+  // decommits kAllowKeepForPerf and then reads the range is fine on Linux,
+  // where the pages stay mapped, and takes a page fault here.
+  const bool change_permissions =
+      accessibility_disposition == PageAccessibilityDisposition::kRequireUpdate;
+  TracePageEvent(change_permissions ? "decommit" : "decommit-keep", address,
+                 length, 0);
   SetProtection(KernelBase, address, length,
                 static_cast<KRN_MapAttr>(MAP_Readable | MAP_Writable));
+  // DiscardSystemPagesInternal() is a no-op on AROS, so this zeroing is what
+  // emulates the discard. Keeping it on both paths means a reader of a
+  // kAllowKeepForPerf range sees zeroes, which is what a POSIX caller gets
+  // back from MADV_DONTNEED.
   std::memset(reinterpret_cast<void*>(address), 0, length);
-  SetProtection(KernelBase, address, length, static_cast<KRN_MapAttr>(0));
+  if (change_permissions) {
+    SetProtection(KernelBase, address, length, static_cast<KRN_MapAttr>(0));
+  }
 }
 
 bool DecommitAndZeroSystemPagesInternal(uintptr_t address,
